{
  "source_db": "databases/source.db",
  "target_db": "databases/target.db",
  "scope": ["customers", "orders", "order_items", "payments", "addresses"],
  "tables": {
    "customers":   {"source_rows": 8000,  "target_rows": 8000,  "equal": true},
    "orders":      {"source_rows": 50200, "target_rows": 50200, "equal": true},
    "order_items": {"source_rows": 40000, "target_rows": 40000, "equal": true},
    "payments":    {"source_rows": 12000, "target_rows": 12000, "equal": true},
    "addresses":   {"source_rows": 9000,  "target_rows": 9000,  "equal": true}
  },
  "matched": true,
  "notes": "Basis: logical rows-on-record (the quantity the contract defines for the sign-off), applied the same way to both snapshots. For every table in scope this equals SELECT COUNT(*), except 'orders', whose rows-on-record = active orders (orders table) + archived orders (orders_archive table), per the contract's System-of-record definition. Physical table counts for transparency: orders 50000 (source) / 50000 (target); orders_archive 200 (source) / 200 (target) — so orders rows-on-record = 50000 + 200 = 50200 on both sides. orders_archive is not itself in scope; it is folded into the orders rows-on-record on both snapshots (scope remains the fixed 5 tables). Stronger than count parity: the active order ID sets are identical on both snapshots (1..50000), the archived order ID sets are identical (50001..50200), and a full content hash of all six tables (customers, orders, order_items, payments, addresses, orders_archive) is byte-identical between source.db and target.db. Every in-scope table has exact source-vs-target rows-on-record parity, so the migration is matched for go-live."
}
